## DESCRIPTION
## Calculus Two, Sequences
## ENDDESCRIPTION


## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Infinite sequences and series)
## DBsection(Limits of sequences)
## Date(09/16/2018)
## Institution(Community College of Denver, Colorado Community College System)
## Author(Brittni Lorton)
## KEYWORDS('calculus', 'sequences', 'formula of a sequence', 'find explicit formula from a recursive formula')



###########################
#  Initialization

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros("PGstandard.pl","PGML.pl","PGcourse.pl");

Context('Numeric')->variables->are(n => "Real");


$a =random(2,5);

$f=Formula("n!/$a^(n-2)");
$f->{test_points} = [[1],[2],[3],[4],[5],[6]];


BEGIN_PGML
Find a function, [`f(n)`], that identifies the [`n`]th term of the following recursively defined sequence as [`a_n=f(n)`].

[```a_1=[$a] \quad\text{and}\quad a_{n+1}=\frac{(n+1)a_n}{[$a]}\quad\text{for}\quad n \geq 1.```]

[`f(n)=`][_]{$ans1}{10}
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Writing out a few terms may help:

[```
\begin{aligned}
a_1 & =[$a] \\
a_2 & = \frac{2}{[$a]} a_1 = \frac{2}{[$a]}\cdot [$a] = 2! \\
a_3 & = \frac{3}{[$a]} a_2 = \frac{2 \cdot 3}{[$a]^2} \cdot [$a] = \frac{3!}{[$a]} \\
a_4 & = \frac{4}{[$a]} a_3 = \frac{2 \cdot 3 \cdot 4}{[$a]^3} \cdot [$a] = \frac{4!}{[$a]^2} \\
a_5 & = \frac{5}{[$a]} a_4 = \frac{2 \cdot 3 \cdot 4 \cdot 5}{[$a]^4} \cdot [$a] = \frac{5!}{[$a]^3}
\end{aligned}```]
so it appears that the sequence can be written as the function:

[``` f(n) = [$f] ```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();